[{:db/id 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1d67db6a-7337-599e-8ecc-26284642ae3b" "question-2c0dea55-217c-592d-9a6c-d305c8e67729"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133711 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ which of the following could be the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\left\\langle -y, \\: y\\right\\rangle$\n\nII. $\\left\\langle -2x, \\: 2x\\right\\rangle$\n\nIII. $\\left\\langle 5y\\sin x, \\: x-5\\cos x \\right\\rangle$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2177a684-6e86-5255-ac01-04a426569ee5" "question-d1f25e51-cfc7-5f63-98bf-d803b12546e2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133712 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2 \\cos t \\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n![](images/45/45513e4aedd37b868d2020cbbb47f49938f6ca03487ded8b5e519a2ec8e4edf5.svg)\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*\n\n![](images/a5/a5eeb5db90f9ee0d86a49e5bbf02629c7886fa019db7cff3c4d0e1eb43e8fd75.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-819104c2-11eb-5b49-9398-d7d5879660f9" "question-bd34f4a5-5a97-5165-88b5-594ad30569e0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1d67db6a-7337-599e-8ecc-26284642ae3b" :question/id #uuid "1d67db6a-7337-599e-8ecc-26284642ae3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126574" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{x},\\,x-{e}^{x}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨2y,\\,x⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{2x}{3}+1⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-{e}^{x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-2{e}^{x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3715f35-6d8a-57c2-a089-fd1bb9bddf47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3715f35-6d8a-57c2-a089-fd1bb9bddf47" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3715f35-6d8a-57c2-a089-fd1bb9bddf47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4798dafc-c73d-5ec4-b12f-da6f6620e4dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f503e12-e11c-54c7-8ba7-5830ef6f73bd" "answer-20a55ace-f5e1-5a5a-91ce-2c0b7d1404a1" "answer-8a583e9f-a399-590a-ac83-6aef2ccb73c6" "answer-fac08602-5de8-5d47-96f6-859f484e607d" "answer-4798dafc-c73d-5ec4-b12f-da6f6620e4dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f503e12-e11c-54c7-8ba7-5830ef6f73bd" :answer/id #uuid "1f503e12-e11c-54c7-8ba7-5830ef6f73bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20a55ace-f5e1-5a5a-91ce-2c0b7d1404a1" :answer/id #uuid "20a55ace-f5e1-5a5a-91ce-2c0b7d1404a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a583e9f-a399-590a-ac83-6aef2ccb73c6" :answer/id #uuid "8a583e9f-a399-590a-ac83-6aef2ccb73c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fac08602-5de8-5d47-96f6-859f484e607d" :answer/id #uuid "fac08602-5de8-5d47-96f6-859f484e607d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4798dafc-c73d-5ec4-b12f-da6f6620e4dd" :answer/id #uuid "4798dafc-c73d-5ec4-b12f-da6f6620e4dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c0dea55-217c-592d-9a6c-d305c8e67729" :question/id #uuid "2c0dea55-217c-592d-9a6c-d305c8e67729" :question/math-academy-id "q-126575" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3},\\,{x}^{3}+1⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{2}+\\sqrt{2}x,\\,\\frac{3x}{2}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨x,\\,x⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{3}{2},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd054105-4a4d-5e12-a937-81dcca4212b8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd054105-4a4d-5e12-a937-81dcca4212b8" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd054105-4a4d-5e12-a937-81dcca4212b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-576c0bfb-effc-5ce5-827d-af6530c65e4e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5d96a71-7dc4-568f-af4e-fef495f9c448" "answer-fcb13b39-dc15-5bed-8347-e772308dd574" "answer-576c0bfb-effc-5ce5-827d-af6530c65e4e" "answer-0de6da70-c426-566d-b3d0-dce7471f5600" "answer-cd786957-3ffe-55c0-a40e-e21f738990d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5d96a71-7dc4-568f-af4e-fef495f9c448" :answer/id #uuid "a5d96a71-7dc4-568f-af4e-fef495f9c448" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcb13b39-dc15-5bed-8347-e772308dd574" :answer/id #uuid "fcb13b39-dc15-5bed-8347-e772308dd574" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-576c0bfb-effc-5ce5-827d-af6530c65e4e" :answer/id #uuid "576c0bfb-effc-5ce5-827d-af6530c65e4e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0de6da70-c426-566d-b3d0-dce7471f5600" :answer/id #uuid "0de6da70-c426-566d-b3d0-dce7471f5600" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd786957-3ffe-55c0-a40e-e21f738990d5" :answer/id #uuid "cd786957-3ffe-55c0-a40e-e21f738990d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2177a684-6e86-5255-ac01-04a426569ee5" :question/id #uuid "2177a684-6e86-5255-ac01-04a426569ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126760" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a95b161dab89fe1245724921ef4ee36112d7a4062e7e171411e4ffe61253b91f.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c9269d5-fd3f-54ff-be8f-682c742d6ce4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c9269d5-fd3f-54ff-be8f-682c742d6ce4" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9269d5-fd3f-54ff-be8f-682c742d6ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f95f8500-94e5-5ec5-b5e0-95f0daf53e67" :answer-field/choices ["answer-31a713ed-2a9a-58c6-bfc8-e17df3a2f279" "answer-a4547936-89a5-5eb1-a5c2-aca7ffed8e29" "answer-f95f8500-94e5-5ec5-b5e0-95f0daf53e67" "answer-30625b21-0638-5df6-8e7f-5ecca67ca674" "answer-7643c9ff-155d-5092-a735-8716263f25b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31a713ed-2a9a-58c6-bfc8-e17df3a2f279" :answer/id #uuid "31a713ed-2a9a-58c6-bfc8-e17df3a2f279" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4547936-89a5-5eb1-a5c2-aca7ffed8e29" :answer/id #uuid "a4547936-89a5-5eb1-a5c2-aca7ffed8e29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f95f8500-94e5-5ec5-b5e0-95f0daf53e67" :answer/id #uuid "f95f8500-94e5-5ec5-b5e0-95f0daf53e67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30625b21-0638-5df6-8e7f-5ecca67ca674" :answer/id #uuid "30625b21-0638-5df6-8e7f-5ecca67ca674" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7643c9ff-155d-5092-a735-8716263f25b6" :answer/id #uuid "7643c9ff-155d-5092-a735-8716263f25b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d1f25e51-cfc7-5f63-98bf-d803b12546e2" :question/id #uuid "d1f25e51-cfc7-5f63-98bf-d803b12546e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126761" :question/problem "![](images/10/10f4094edb09afc7908bd0cd3df089387c8f5b762ea5ef86da152d1c9d08b5e4.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{sin}^{5}⁡t,\\,{cos}^{5}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨5{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{sin}^{5}⁡t)\\cdot (-5{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{sin}^{6}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7e0a66d-4420-5ec9-9e88-13a5cdd58112"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7e0a66d-4420-5ec9-9e88-13a5cdd58112" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e0a66d-4420-5ec9-9e88-13a5cdd58112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f58f79ef-051d-5166-b8fb-70ef66a978d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7bf73cdb-b201-56ec-9e65-ae9499b1e5c5" "answer-e1ec8ccb-1292-5505-905e-84a2ec107ae8" "answer-88f605ff-e892-57f0-8a1b-c76ad9f5a91e" "answer-031791c1-0f2f-546c-82e8-999c8554dc97" "answer-f58f79ef-051d-5166-b8fb-70ef66a978d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bf73cdb-b201-56ec-9e65-ae9499b1e5c5" :answer/id #uuid "7bf73cdb-b201-56ec-9e65-ae9499b1e5c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5{cos}^{5}⁡t{sin}^{5}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1ec8ccb-1292-5505-905e-84a2ec107ae8" :answer/id #uuid "e1ec8ccb-1292-5505-905e-84a2ec107ae8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5{sin}^{6}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88f605ff-e892-57f0-8a1b-c76ad9f5a91e" :answer/id #uuid "88f605ff-e892-57f0-8a1b-c76ad9f5a91e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{cos}^{5}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-031791c1-0f2f-546c-82e8-999c8554dc97" :answer/id #uuid "031791c1-0f2f-546c-82e8-999c8554dc97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{cos}^{6}⁡t{sin}^{4}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f58f79ef-051d-5166-b8fb-70ef66a978d8" :answer/id #uuid "f58f79ef-051d-5166-b8fb-70ef66a978d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{sin}^{6}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-819104c2-11eb-5b49-9398-d7d5879660f9" :question/id #uuid "819104c2-11eb-5b49-9398-d7d5879660f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126727" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b51e252935f7077f3285258845d8d1e4957b83894037f968ee306910aa88bad6.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-771735d3-fa1a-5273-a935-b05b06edb2c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-771735d3-fa1a-5273-a935-b05b06edb2c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "771735d3-fa1a-5273-a935-b05b06edb2c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4addbefd-201e-5755-8641-7e27605c3311" "answer-dd351131-c322-54cb-8179-38615de2670f" "answer-b4200cd7-229f-5b2c-80d8-6fbc6195eb39" "answer-46ee80ff-c5ae-55ec-a96c-b506acfb7915" "answer-7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4addbefd-201e-5755-8641-7e27605c3311" :answer/id #uuid "4addbefd-201e-5755-8641-7e27605c3311" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd351131-c322-54cb-8179-38615de2670f" :answer/id #uuid "dd351131-c322-54cb-8179-38615de2670f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4200cd7-229f-5b2c-80d8-6fbc6195eb39" :answer/id #uuid "b4200cd7-229f-5b2c-80d8-6fbc6195eb39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46ee80ff-c5ae-55ec-a96c-b506acfb7915" :answer/id #uuid "46ee80ff-c5ae-55ec-a96c-b506acfb7915" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9" :answer/id #uuid "7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bd34f4a5-5a97-5165-88b5-594ad30569e0" :question/id #uuid "bd34f4a5-5a97-5165-88b5-594ad30569e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126859" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ebabcf1e3c3c3d2f4850d425657e7314c0135c81a432bfbbc8ae31ee39cb85d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{5}\\,i-2{t}^{4}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-t\\,i-2\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}\\,i-8{t}^{3}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}x\\,dy-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{9}}{9}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-64517546-ac9e-59a3-822e-98cda097a61b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-64517546-ac9e-59a3-822e-98cda097a61b" :answer-field/id #uuid "64517546-ac9e-59a3-822e-98cda097a61b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b151d74-5c74-545d-8b07-dea1d8453657" "answer-419ff17a-c941-551d-ad1c-89eccf44a609" "answer-a3a3e7c7-ac4d-53f3-be35-8650eb6afd4d" "answer-32eb8a32-3dbf-536c-8383-dfb162184c29" "answer-4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b151d74-5c74-545d-8b07-dea1d8453657" :answer/id #uuid "2b151d74-5c74-545d-8b07-dea1d8453657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-419ff17a-c941-551d-ad1c-89eccf44a609" :answer/id #uuid "419ff17a-c941-551d-ad1c-89eccf44a609" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3a3e7c7-ac4d-53f3-be35-8650eb6afd4d" :answer/id #uuid "a3a3e7c7-ac4d-53f3-be35-8650eb6afd4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32eb8a32-3dbf-536c-8383-dfb162184c29" :answer/id #uuid "32eb8a32-3dbf-536c-8383-dfb162184c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50" :answer/id #uuid "4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135131 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135132 :tutorial/content "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135137 :tutorial/content "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =  1,\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134040 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
